&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:31.035
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:36.711
&gt;&gt; So, it's good to be here.

00:01:36.711 --> 00:01:42.711
I guess, I heard from the reactions
to the Deans calling out the years,

00:01:42.711 --> 00:01:49.020
I have some idea of how many of you
are here from 2012 and from 1998.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:49.520
And welcome back.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.070
I also returned this year.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:57.372
As I mentioned, I got my PhD in 1974,
my advisor was Bob Wilson.

00:01:57.372 --> 00:02:00.169
How many of you had a class
with Bob when he was here?

00:02:00.169 --> 00:02:02.538
Well, I hope you appreciated it.

00:02:02.538 --> 00:02:07.942
He started market design,
he's one of the giants in that field.

00:02:07.942 --> 00:02:12.842
And I'm going to tell you what's
happened to it since then because it's

00:02:12.842 --> 00:02:17.990
grown quite a bit since it's
beginnings here at Stanford.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:24.570
So what market designers do
is think about, what market.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.450
It might better be called
market place design.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.920
We think about what markets
doing what market places

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.650
have to accomplish in order
to make markets work well.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.590
And so, what I want to do today,
in just tongue twist is to think a little

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:38.930
bit about what market places are because
you are out in the market now.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:43.880
But markets are a lot of different
things and I want to spend

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:48.300
the time together tonight talking about
how markets work and how they fail.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.860
And how we can fix them
when they're broken.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.520
And how markets may be a more

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.030
comprehensive class of institutions than
you're accustomed to thinking about.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.610
And so
I'll tell you a little about my work,

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:05.290
and I'll focus on the parts that are least
like the market you are familiar with.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.933
And so let me start by asking,
what's the role of money in markets?

00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:16.430
So here's a food market and
the New York Stock Exchange.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.010
And mostly when we think about markets,
we thing about commodity markets.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.921
And in commodity markets,
the role of money is huge.

00:03:23.921 --> 00:03:26.620
Thus supply and
demand decide who gets what, so

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.941
the thing about commodity markets is if
you are making small enough trades in

00:03:30.941 --> 00:03:34.000
a big enough market,
you don't particularly care.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.260
You don't have to care
who you're dealing with.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.430
When you buy 100 shares of AT&amp;T
on the New York Stock Exchange,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.610
you don't have to fill
out any application form.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:44.950
You don't care who the seller is, you're
not worried about whether he's taking

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.940
good care of those shares while he
had them, and he's not going to

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.970
worry about whether you'll take good
care of them while you have them.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:53.260
This is an arms length
anonymous transaction, and

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.350
the job of the New York Stock Exchange
is price discovery.

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.020
It's to find the price at which
supply will equal demand, and

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.940
that's the price at which you'll transact.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.900
But in many markets prices
don't do all the work.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.420
And you're all familiar
with these markets,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.050
although we don't always
think of them that way.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:14.640
For instance, Stanford doesn't fill
its MBA class by raising tuition until

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.320
just enough people are ready
to come to Stanford, right?

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.600
Stanford is expensive to go to, but

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.410
it's cheap enough that lots
of people would like to come.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:27.670
And so Stanford said to tuition,
get's lots of applicants and

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.960
then chooses which ones can come.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:34.648
So the price doesn't decide
who gets into Stanford, okay?

00:04:34.648 --> 00:04:37.505
The labor markets are like that too.

00:04:37.505 --> 00:04:42.003
When you go out on the job market, it may
sometimes seem as if your company lowers

00:04:42.003 --> 00:04:46.160
the wage until just enough people
remain who want to do the work.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.910
But that's probably not
the way they do that in

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:50.960
most of the companies that you work for.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.417
So instead,
they get lots of applications and

00:04:53.417 --> 00:04:55.750
they have to choose who
to make the offer to.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.326
And of course just as Stanford
can't choose who comes,

00:04:59.326 --> 00:05:01.160
firms can't chose either.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.960
You can't choose to go there, but
they can't choose that you come there.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.576
They have to compete with
the other offers you're getting.

00:05:05.576 --> 00:05:07.642
Stanford has to compete with Harvard.

00:05:07.642 --> 00:05:13.222
So, matching markets are the markets in
which you can't just choose what you want,

00:05:13.222 --> 00:05:15.360
even if you can afford it.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.090
You also have to be chosen, right?

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.578
You can't just decide to come to Stanford.

00:05:20.578 --> 00:05:21.425
You have to be admitted.

00:05:21.425 --> 00:05:25.443
You can't just decide to show up for
work at Google, you have to be hired.

00:05:25.443 --> 00:05:28.347
But Stanford can't just decide
that you'll come either, and

00:05:28.347 --> 00:05:30.273
Google can't decide that you'll come.

00:05:30.273 --> 00:05:33.338
So these are matching
markets that have courtship.

00:05:33.338 --> 00:05:37.459
They're a lot like marriage, right?

00:05:37.459 --> 00:05:40.502
You can't just choose your spouse,
you also have to be chosen.

00:05:40.502 --> 00:05:44.426
And a lot of the marketplace institutions
are about that courtship there.

00:05:44.426 --> 00:05:48.330
Applications and admissions,
and selections, and

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.940
visiting days, and recruitment.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:56.025
So I've been involved with my colleagues
in a bunch of matching markets.

00:05:56.025 --> 00:06:00.532
And some of them use money, and
some of them don't use money at all.

00:06:00.532 --> 00:06:02.560
And I'll focus today on
some of the stranger ones,

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:04.268
on one of the stranger ones that doesn't.

00:06:04.268 --> 00:06:08.431
But even the ones that use money,
the labor markets, for

00:06:08.431 --> 00:06:12.267
instance, that isn't what
decides who gets what.

00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:16.940
So the thing about matching markets
is just about every market that

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.370
isn't a commodity market
is a matching market.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.542
You care about who
you're transacting with.

00:06:22.542 --> 00:06:24.113
And the price, therefore,

00:06:24.113 --> 00:06:27.593
isn't the only thing that decides
what happens in the market.

00:06:27.593 --> 00:06:32.429
So my colleagues and I have designed a lot
of labor market clearing houses, for

00:06:32.429 --> 00:06:33.170
instance.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.300
If you have friends who are doctors,

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.505
they go through an unusual job market when
they graduate from medical school called

00:06:39.505 --> 00:06:44.675
the National Resident Matching Program and
that's something that I go to design.

00:06:44.675 --> 00:06:47.685
We've made some innovations for
new PhD economist recently.

00:06:47.685 --> 00:06:49.937
That's one that's close to home.

00:06:49.937 --> 00:06:52.863
Those are both markets where
of course money is used.

00:06:52.863 --> 00:06:56.045
Everyone who is a doctor or
is a Professor of Economics gets paid.

00:06:56.045 --> 00:06:59.745
But it's not the pay that decides
who's a professor at Stanford or

00:06:59.745 --> 00:07:02.002
who's a Doctor at Stanford Hospitals.

00:07:02.002 --> 00:07:05.869
Now there are other matching markets,
where we don't use money at all because we

00:07:05.869 --> 00:07:08.537
don't think it would be
an appropriate thing to do.

00:07:08.537 --> 00:07:13.570
So school choice systems for who goes
to which public schools, all right?

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:14.947
We think as a society,

00:07:14.947 --> 00:07:19.630
we think we have an obligation to
provide education to our children.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:23.780
And public school is mandatory and
public school is free.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:26.620
But there's still our issues of choice,
who goes to which school.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:31.323
And my colleagues and I have gotten to
design choice systems for New York City

00:07:31.323 --> 00:07:36.830
high schools and for Boston public schools
and recently in Denver and New Orleans.

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:41.047
And just last week we hosted a conference
of many different school districts here

00:07:41.047 --> 00:07:42.544
to talk about these things.

00:07:42.544 --> 00:07:45.229
And so
I imagine that some of the technology,

00:07:45.229 --> 00:07:50.103
some of the kinds of things that I'll tell
you a little bit about, when I talk about

00:07:50.103 --> 00:07:55.212
kidney exchange are going to diffuse into
more American cities, maybe one near you.

00:07:55.212 --> 00:07:59.886
But what I'm going to you about
mostly today is kidney exchange.

00:07:59.886 --> 00:08:02.732
Kidney exchange is a market
where no money is used.

00:08:02.732 --> 00:08:04.414
But after I tell you about it,

00:08:04.414 --> 00:08:08.197
I hope you'll see why I think of
it as being clearly a marketplace.

00:08:08.197 --> 00:08:11.239
And so that's really
the subtext of my talk today,

00:08:11.239 --> 00:08:15.717
is how things that you might not think
of as markets act like marketplaces.

00:08:15.717 --> 00:08:17.538
And we should think of them as markets.

00:08:17.538 --> 00:08:23.050
And again, many, many of the most
important markets are matching markets.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:25.700
So before I go on to
tell you about kidneys,

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:29.600
think about all the matching
that you have all gone through.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:35.830
At some point you got into schools at
various levels, you got into college.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:38.870
These are things that cost
a lot of money sometimes but

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.250
the money doesn't
determine who goes where.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.208
Then you're all graduates of Stanford.

00:08:44.208 --> 00:08:45.594
You've all gotten jobs.

00:08:45.594 --> 00:08:47.208
Some of you have created your own jobs.

00:08:47.208 --> 00:08:50.670
But some of you have gotten
jobs with existing firms.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.400
Many of you have,
those are matching markets.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.530
Many of you are married.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:57.802
Some of you who aren't married or

00:08:57.802 --> 00:08:59.625
are going to be married
sometime in the future.

00:08:59.625 --> 00:09:04.381
You're engaged in a kind of marriage
market where you can't just choose what

00:09:04.381 --> 00:09:06.733
you want, you also have to be chosen.

00:09:06.733 --> 00:09:10.046
And I'm going to talk to you about some
aspects of medical care where you can't

00:09:10.046 --> 00:09:12.452
just choose what you want,
but also have to be chosen.

00:09:12.452 --> 00:09:15.310
In particular, I'm going to talk
about organ transplantation.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:21.454
So this is a good example of
the allocation of scarce resources, so

00:09:21.454 --> 00:09:26.684
it's a natural topic for economists but
it's without the use of money

00:09:26.684 --> 00:09:32.999
because in most of the West we have laws
against buying organs for transplantation.

00:09:32.999 --> 00:09:37.880
And it's a big part of the economy,
although you can't buy a kidney,

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:42.847
the care of patients with end stage
renal disease with Kidney disease is

00:09:42.847 --> 00:09:45.445
a multi-billion dollar industry.

00:09:45.445 --> 00:09:47.740
So, this is an industry
with lots of interest.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:51.100
It's not insignificant part
of health care expenditures;

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.190
it's the biggest single
program in Medicare.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.811
So, many billions of
dollars are spent on this.

00:09:58.811 --> 00:10:02.892
And, the unusual thing about
transplantation is that it is not only

00:10:02.892 --> 00:10:06.544
the treatment of choice,
it is not only the best treatment,

00:10:06.544 --> 00:10:08.057
it is also the cheapest.

00:10:08.057 --> 00:10:13.138
So dialysis is very expensive it costs
Medicare about $85,000 a year to keep

00:10:13.138 --> 00:10:18.584
someone on dialysis, but it only costs
$120,000 for someone to get a transplant

00:10:18.584 --> 00:10:23.990
after which they need immunosuppressive
drugs that cost about $20,000 a year.

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:25.370
And they get up and go back to work and

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:28.620
something that when they are on
dialysis after a while they can't do.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:31.670
So, this is not an insignificant
part of the economy.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:35.960
It's a part of the economy that
has very scarce resources.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:40.706
I'll tell you how we try to get more
transplants in a marketplace that plays

00:10:40.706 --> 00:10:42.226
out without any money.

00:10:42.226 --> 00:10:45.070
Although there's important
money in the background.

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.688
So what's the story with kidney exchange?

00:10:47.688 --> 00:10:51.920
So many more people need kidneys
than there are organs available.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.770
So there are 95,000 people on
the deceased donor waiting list

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:59.331
at the moment in the United States.

00:10:59.331 --> 00:11:03.330
They're waiting for
an organ from a deceased donor.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:07.050
So when you signed up for
a California driver's license,

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.770
when you were here, if you did that,

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:12.510
you had an opportunity to register on
the California organ donor registry.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:16.250
That's where deceased donors
mostly come from, that registry.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:19.180
So the waiting list has 100,000 people.

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:23.170
That means you might have to wait a long
time because we only have about 11,000

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.235
deceased donors in
the United States a year.

00:11:25.235 --> 00:11:31.937
11,000 deceased donor transplants,
that's only about 6,000 deceased donors.

00:11:31.937 --> 00:11:35.450
But deceased donors aren't
the only source of transplants.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:39.926
You each have two kidneys, and if you're
as healthy as I hope you are then if

00:11:39.926 --> 00:11:43.916
you knew someone who had kidney
disease you can give them a kidney.

00:11:43.916 --> 00:11:46.930
because if you're healthy,
you can remain healthy with one kidney.

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:52.670
So you might be healthy enough to
give someone you love a kidney, but

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:57.379
the might still not be able to take your
kidney, and that's why the possibility of

00:11:57.379 --> 00:12:01.973
exchange comes in which of course is
a natural subject for economists to study.

00:12:01.973 --> 00:12:06.037
So the idea of exchange is you
want to give a kidney to someone,

00:12:06.037 --> 00:12:09.008
in this case supposing you're donor 1, but

00:12:09.008 --> 00:12:13.460
your potential recipient has
a different blood type than you do.

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:14.436
So blood type A and blood type B.

00:12:14.436 --> 00:12:17.559
So you'd like to give recipient
1 a kidney but you can't,

00:12:17.559 --> 00:12:20.000
she's got a different
blood type than you do.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.820
And donor 2 and
recipient 2 are in the opposite situation.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:29.240
So an exchange is possible where donor
1 the blood type A donor gives a kidney

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.320
recipient 2 the blood type A recipient.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:32.982
Everyone gets a compatible kidney.

00:12:32.982 --> 00:12:38.050
This kind of thing happens for other
reasons than blood typing compatibility.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:43.471
Turns out the chance that one of you could
take my kidney is a little over 50%.

00:12:43.471 --> 00:12:48.373
The chance that my wife can take my kidney
is only about 30% and that's because my

00:12:48.373 --> 00:12:53.066
wife and I are parents and in the course
of childbirth, my wife's immune system

00:12:53.066 --> 00:12:58.060
might have been exposed to some of the
proteins that our boys inherit from me.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:01.242
And if so, she might have antibodies to
those proteins that would be ready to

00:13:01.242 --> 00:13:02.843
attack my kidney if it should show up.

00:13:02.843 --> 00:13:08.540
So there are other kinds incompatibilities
than just blood type incompatibility.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.057
And someone who's been sick for
a long time or who's had a previous kidney

00:13:12.057 --> 00:13:15.008
transplant will have lots of
antibodies to human proteins and

00:13:15.008 --> 00:13:18.431
they'll be hard to match, whereas
other people will be easy to match.

00:13:18.431 --> 00:13:21.439
I just came back from Korea,
which is a place actually,

00:13:21.439 --> 00:13:24.447
where kidney exchange has been
going on for some time and

00:13:24.447 --> 00:13:27.800
the issue there is in Korea,
but not in the United States.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:30.713
Blood types A and
B are equally frequent, so

00:13:30.713 --> 00:13:34.593
this kind of simple exchange
can go on quite easily there.

00:13:34.593 --> 00:13:38.150
Okay, now notice that no money
changed hands in that exchange.

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:41.152
And one, there are many reasons for
that, but

00:13:41.152 --> 00:13:42.948
one reason is it's against the law, okay?

00:13:42.948 --> 00:13:48.033
So here's a sentence from the 1984
National Organ Transplant Act that says,

00:13:48.033 --> 00:13:52.157
it shall be unlawful for
any person to knowingly acquire it, etc,

00:13:52.157 --> 00:13:57.890
any human organ for valuable consideration
for use in human transplantation.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:01.763
So you might worry that, that's going
to rule out kidney exchange, too.

00:14:01.763 --> 00:14:04.680
That sure looks like valuable
considerations going on there.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:06.567
A kidney's being exchanged for a kidney.

00:14:06.567 --> 00:14:09.705
And if you get very
philosophical about this but

00:14:09.705 --> 00:14:15.278
eventually we got an amendment to the note
that simply said the preceding sentence,

00:14:15.278 --> 00:14:19.140
that one,
doesn't apply to kidney exchange.

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:22.020
I learned something about federal
legislation while this was going on.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:23.205
There was no opposition to this.

00:14:23.205 --> 00:14:25.344
No one opposes kidney exchange, but

00:14:25.344 --> 00:14:29.470
it still took three Congresses
before this bill passed.

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:35.440
And it didn't pass until Charlie Norwood,
the representative from Georgia who's name

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:41.010
is on the bill now, died of
a immunosuppressive related disease.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:44.192
He had a transplant and you take
immunosuppressant drugs forever after, so

00:14:44.192 --> 00:14:46.140
you're vulnerable to
some kind of diseases.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:50.070
So it went down just out of
neglect in two Congresses.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:50.990
In the third Congress,

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:54.280
Charlie Norwood died and his colleagues
named the bill after him and passed it.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:57.052
It passed by a claim with no dissent, so

00:14:57.052 --> 00:15:01.427
legislation there are some
frictions out there in the world.

00:15:01.427 --> 00:15:03.954
Okay, so let me tell you
about some other frictions,

00:15:03.954 --> 00:15:06.510
about the actual frictions
in running an exchange.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:09.714
So here's a picture of an exchange,
I'm the man in the yellow gown.

00:15:09.714 --> 00:15:15.069
You can tell that I'm keeping my hands out
of the way so no one hands me or anything.

00:15:15.069 --> 00:15:15.841
And-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:15.841 --> 00:15:17.894
&gt;&gt; And what I would like to not be handed

00:15:17.894 --> 00:15:20.910
is in this bucket here,
there's a kidney in there.

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:23.421
And behind me, not in this picture but

00:15:23.421 --> 00:15:27.668
behind me in this picture off
screen is another operating room.

00:15:27.668 --> 00:15:31.232
And the kidney has just been removed,
just steps away and carried over into this

00:15:31.232 --> 00:15:34.780
operating room where it's being
transplanted into this patient.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:38.449
And this is all happening in Cincinnati,
Ohio.

00:15:38.449 --> 00:15:43.765
And at the same time in Toledo,
Ohio the donor, her patient and his,

00:15:43.765 --> 00:15:50.010
the transplant recipient, his donor
are doing the same thing in Toledo, Ohio.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:53.601
And when I say at the same time, I mean
literally at the same time because you

00:15:53.601 --> 00:15:57.705
can't write a contract on a kidney because
no valuable consideration can be given for

00:15:57.705 --> 00:15:58.225
a kidney.

00:15:58.225 --> 00:16:02.540
So what they do in this kind of exchange
is they They prep the patients,

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:04.960
they anesthetize them,
they make the initial incisions.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:06.710
Then they get on their cell phones, and

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:09.959
they say we're ready to go here in
Cincinnati, are you ready in Toledo?

00:16:09.959 --> 00:16:14.045
And when they're told that everything went
smoothly in the preparations in Toledo,

00:16:14.045 --> 00:16:18.150
then they go ahead and they take the
kidney and everything proceeds from there.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:23.770
So this kind of simultaneous
exchange requires for

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:27.175
surgeries, at the same time,
two nephrectomies, taking the kidney out,

00:16:27.175 --> 00:16:29.330
and two transplants,
putting the kidney in.

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:32.820
That means it takes a lot of
resources to do this simple exchange.

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:36.470
You need four operating rooms and
four surgical teams to do it.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:39.600
So even the simplest
two-way exchange is hard.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.990
It involves a lot of resources.

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:45.968
And we'd like to do other kinds of
exchanges because if you're restricted to

00:16:45.968 --> 00:16:49.430
two-way exchanges, you don't get as
many transplants as you could otherwise.

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:52.139
This way, we get two transplants that
wouldn't otherwise have happened.

00:16:52.139 --> 00:16:58.955
So that's great, two live donor organs
are donated to patients who need them.

00:16:58.955 --> 00:17:02.105
But sometimes,
you can't find two-way exchanges.

00:17:02.105 --> 00:17:04.645
So sometimes you'd like to be
able to do a three-way exchange.

00:17:04.645 --> 00:17:07.115
For instance, here's three pairs.

00:17:07.115 --> 00:17:11.710
Each pair can give a kidney to the patient
in the next pair in a three-way cycle.

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:15.690
So that would require six operating
rooms and six surgical teams.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.810
And over time,
we've become able to do that.

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:21.085
We can assemble those logistics,
but that's about our limit.

00:17:21.085 --> 00:17:23.422
And every now and then,
a larger exchange gets done.

00:17:23.422 --> 00:17:27.907
But the logistics of getting everybody in
place simultaneously to do a three-way

00:17:27.907 --> 00:17:29.929
exchange is about all we can manage.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:32.375
And it`s still not enough.

00:17:32.375 --> 00:17:35.448
So the question is,
what can you do in this marketplace that

00:17:35.448 --> 00:17:39.070
we're creating to facilitate
exchange where exchanges are hard?

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:43.250
Large exchanges are hard because
of a simultaneity requirement.

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:48.680
Well, it turns out, so here's a picture,
where, think of each circle as being

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:55.130
typically a pair, a patient donor pair,
and an arrow goes from one circle to

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:59.060
another if a kidney from that pair can
go to the patient in the other pair.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:02.140
Okay, so you see that there's a three-way
exchange is possible here and

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:03.080
a two-way exchange.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:04.871
There's also a non-directed donor.

00:18:04.871 --> 00:18:08.207
So this is a donor who doesn't
have a particular patient in mind.

00:18:08.207 --> 00:18:12.211
And we can sometimes get some chains
going, where he'll give a kidney to some

00:18:12.211 --> 00:18:16.334
pair, and the donor in that pair will give
a kidney to a patient in the next pair,

00:18:16.334 --> 00:18:17.610
and so forth.

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:19.150
So let's think about those
chains a little bit.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:25.839
When non-directed donors first
started to show up, so ten years ago,

00:18:25.839 --> 00:18:27.512
they were pretty rare.

00:18:27.512 --> 00:18:30.441
What we used to say to them after,
first of all,

00:18:30.441 --> 00:18:35.280
they would be any donor gets a thorough
physical exam and psychological exam,

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:39.192
make sure they know what they're doing and
they're willing.

00:18:39.192 --> 00:18:43.290
And non-directed donors ten years ago
were treated with a certain suspicion.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.503
Sort of the catch-22 of surgery was,

00:18:46.503 --> 00:18:51.613
if you want to give someone a kidney,
maybe you were unbalanced.

00:18:51.613 --> 00:18:53.990
But nowadays,
we've seen many more of these.

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:57.660
Just incidentally,
a big group of non-directed donors,

00:18:57.660 --> 00:18:59.810
when you look at the non-directed
donors in the United States,

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:02.630
a big group of them come from
faith-based organizations.

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:04.550
The second big group are policemen and
firemen.

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:10.250
They turn out to save people,
and they're not afraid.

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:13.967
But we used to say to them,
is we used to say, well,

00:19:13.967 --> 00:19:16.340
you have passed through all the screens.

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:17.780
You could give a kidney to a person,

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:22.070
to a patient on the waiting list, somebody
waiting for a long time for a kidney.

00:19:22.070 --> 00:19:24.730
A live donor kidney is better
than a deceased donor kidney,

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:26.230
you'll save their life.

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:29.404
And that way, you would get an extra
transplant that you wouldn't otherwise.

00:19:29.404 --> 00:19:32.287
But now that we have this
pool of patient donor pairs,

00:19:32.287 --> 00:19:36.500
we can say to non-directed donors,
we can say you could start a chain.

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:39.965
You could give a kidney to
a patient who has a donor,

00:19:39.965 --> 00:19:43.926
who would give a kidney to
another patient who has a donor.

00:19:43.926 --> 00:19:48.830
And that donor could give to
a patient on the waiting list.

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:50.685
So this is a sort of
abstract look at that,

00:19:50.685 --> 00:19:53.494
this is what it looks like when
you take a picture of the people.

00:19:53.494 --> 00:20:00.599
And one question is, why are there
only six people in that picture?

00:20:00.599 --> 00:20:03.865
And the reason that there are only six
people in the picture is we can only

00:20:03.865 --> 00:20:05.945
muster six operating rooms at a time,
okay?

00:20:05.945 --> 00:20:09.362
So as long as we're doing
these chains simultaneously,

00:20:09.362 --> 00:20:11.435
this is as big as the picture gets.

00:20:11.435 --> 00:20:14.380
So let's think again
about this marketplace.

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:18.520
Why do we do them simultaneously, and
could we relax that in some circumstances?

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:21.410
because if you could do it not
simultaneously, you could use

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:25.810
the same surgical teams and the same
operating rooms over at different times.

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:28.040
And you could get more
people into the chain.

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:30.340
So let's think about why
we do it simultaneously.

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:34.907
So let's go back to that simple exchange
that I showed you about initially between

00:20:34.907 --> 00:20:36.380
two pairs.

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:39.840
And suppose we always do these
simultaneously, let's see why.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:44.228
Supposing we didn't do it simultaneously,
suppose the idea was that on day one,

00:20:44.228 --> 00:20:47.039
pair donor two would give
a kidney to recipient one.

00:20:47.039 --> 00:20:50.540
So he doesn't have a kidney anymore,
he's successfully given that kidney.

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:54.490
And on day two, donor one is supposed
to give a kidney to recipient two, but

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:59.550
something goes wrong, and
that link breaks and it doesn't happen.

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:02.307
Well, that's a tragedy for pair two.

00:21:02.307 --> 00:21:04.800
They've had a surgery
that didn't help them.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:10.190
They've given a kidney and not gotten one,
and they don't have a kidney anymore.

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:13.150
They can't participate in
kidney exchange next week

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:15.620
because they don't have
a kidney to exchange.

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:19.450
So they've been tragically harmed,
so we never do that.

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:21.370
We always do that simultaneously.

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.900
But now suppose a living
non-directed donor shows up.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:27.460
And let's think about doing
that non-simultaneously.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:32.010
So on day one, the non-directed donor
gives to recipient one successfully.

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:36.460
And on day two, the donor one
fails to give to recipient two for

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:39.440
whatever reason, just same reason
it was here, whatever it was.

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:42.540
Well, that's really disappointing.

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.450
Recipient two didn't get a kidney.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.090
But it's not tragic.

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:47.880
The pair two,
they haven't given a kidney yet.

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:48.680
They still have a kidney.

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:50.840
They can take part in
kidney exchange next week.

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:54.250
And they're no worse off than they were
before the non-directed donor showed up.

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.530
So they're disappointed, but
they're not really harmed.

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.120
The cost of a broken link is much less.

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:02.460
So we can start to explore
whether the benefit

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:05.630
of doing things non-simultaneously
might be worth the risk.

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:09.930
We'd have to discover how big the risk
is and how big the benefit is.

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:13.532
So the first non-simultaneous
exchange we did was in 2007.

00:22:13.532 --> 00:22:16.717
It was reported in this paper in 2009, and

00:22:16.717 --> 00:22:21.464
the first author is Mike Rees,
he's one of the heroes of this story.

00:22:21.464 --> 00:22:24.890
He's the kidney surgeon in Toledo, Ohio.

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:28.057
There's a bunch of economists and
computer scientists in here, so

00:22:28.057 --> 00:22:29.620
it's an unusual medical paper.

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:33.152
And that first non-simultaneous
chain had ten transplants,

00:22:33.152 --> 00:22:35.090
it had 20 people in the picture.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.362
Okay, here's the better picture,

00:22:38.362 --> 00:22:41.990
as it showed up in
People Magazine some time later.

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:45.550
Okay, so that's a much bigger picture.

00:22:45.550 --> 00:22:47.385
So what's going on there?

00:22:47.385 --> 00:22:52.290
Here's a market place that we've now
opened up a new kind of exchange,

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:55.491
and we're starting to
see more transplants.

00:22:55.491 --> 00:22:58.470
Well, what's going on?

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:01.859
It turns out that when we
started kidney exchange, and

00:23:01.859 --> 00:23:06.722
no one knew anything about it, the
patients we got were ordinary sick people,

00:23:06.722 --> 00:23:09.170
ordinary people with kidney disease.

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:11.285
And some of them were pretty
easy to match to each other,

00:23:11.285 --> 00:23:13.510
and some of them were hard to match.

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:17.286
As in the last ten years, as kidney
exchange has grown in the United States,

00:23:17.286 --> 00:23:19.412
transplant centers have learned to do it.

00:23:19.412 --> 00:23:23.920
And they're now often withholding
their easy to match pairs.

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.779
So if people come in and
they're like those two pairs I showed you,

00:23:26.779 --> 00:23:28.681
often we never see them
in kidney exchange.

00:23:28.681 --> 00:23:30.580
They just get transplanted
in their hospital.

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:33.187
It means that the patients we
are seeing are hard to match.

00:23:33.187 --> 00:23:34.870
What does it mean to be hard to match?

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:36.580
It means you've got a lot of antibodies.

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:38.620
So there aren't many kidneys you can take.

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:43.670
When we draw those arrows between circles,
there aren't so many arrows, okay?

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:48.970
So here's a bit of data from
one of our kidney exchanges.

00:23:48.970 --> 00:23:51.920
And you can see there are these guys
in blue who have a lot of arrows

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:53.200
pointing into them.

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:56.490
They can take a lot of kidneys,
they're the low sensitized patients.

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.320
But there's lots of people who,
although they are donors can give

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:03.570
kidneys to the easy to match patients,
they can't get kidneys from any people.

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:05.270
So this graph's a little hard to look at.

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:08.692
So what I want you to think about is
supposing all those things are sort of

00:24:08.692 --> 00:24:12.510
strings and you grab the blue,
nodes in your hands and you shake it.

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:15.050
You're going to get a picture
that looks like this jellyfish.

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:17.510
And at the top we've got
the easy to match pairs, and

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:20.390
they're sort of in a densely
connected part of the graph.

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:22.420
Lots of exchanges can go on there.

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:25.100
And then there are these
chains hanging down.

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:28.710
And it's those chains that we're getting
in these long, simultaneous chains.

00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:31.650
If you got a non-directed donor you can
start at the bottom of one of those chains

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:35.340
and just move it right up and
some of those chains can be pretty long.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:38.520
So, here's a chain that has
sixty people in it, okay?

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:43.680
The very first one is an undirected donor
and the others are all hard to match.

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:48.450
Patients who, who we couldn't
take care of in small exchanges.

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:51.330
So 60 people in the picture
means 30 transplants.

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:53.150
30 donors and 30 transplants, okay.

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:56.230
So that's basically what we're seeing now.

00:24:56.230 --> 00:25:00.540
This is the fastest growing
part of organ transplantation.

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:01.830
And it's exciting to see.

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:05.189
But let's think a little about this.

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:09.920
Why don't we just buy and
sell kidneys, right?

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:12.468
There you know, when you talk
to Gary Becker at the University

00:25:12.468 --> 00:25:15.510
of Chicago he says there's no shortage
of kidneys, you've all got two.

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:19.620
You know this room has a huge excess
of kidneys because you only need one.

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:24.940
Okay, so I don't know the answer to, to
why we don't allow buy and sell kidneys,

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:28.670
but it's certainly something that
economists and business people ought to

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:32.600
know more about because, there are lots of
things we don't allow you to buy and sell.

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:37.080
And that reflects the fact that people
have, you know, ordinary people,

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:38.990
not economists, not business people,

00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:43.580
have intuitions about what's
the proper scope of markets.

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:47.820
We probably have some of those
intuitions ourselves and

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:50.580
one reason to think about it is because
making markets illegally doesn't

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:52.540
necessarily stop them
from happening right?

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:54.090
So there are lots of things
that you can't buy and

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:58.260
sell in the United States and
there are black markets for many of them.

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:00.370
Think of illegal drugs for example.

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:03.230
So let's think about this a little bit.

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:06.780
And when I started to think about
this I needed a word that would

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:10.500
say what I wanted so
I borrowed the word repugnant.

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:14.780
When I say a transaction is repugnant
I'm not thinking of it's simple meaning,

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:15.860
that you don't like it.

00:26:15.860 --> 00:26:19.020
What I mean is,
I'll say a transaction is repugnant

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:23.006
if some people would like to engage in
it and other people don't want them to.

00:26:23.006 --> 00:26:28.109
Okay, so the has been really important,

00:26:28.109 --> 00:26:33.270
historically, it's important presently.

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:36.950
Here we are in California I think
a prototypical repugnant transaction,

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.930
the way I am using the term,
is same-sex marriage, right.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:43.850
A repugnant transaction is a transaction
that some people want to engage in and

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:45.360
other people don't want them to.

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:50.080
So right now if Rhode Island changes its
law, which I think they are about to.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:54.440
We will have 10 states and the district
of Columbia that allow same-sex marriage.

00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:55.920
And the rest of the states don't.

00:26:55.920 --> 00:26:58.510
And right now California is
one of the ones that don't.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:02.550
Although for the Supreme Court
they've already heard oral arguments

00:27:02.550 --> 00:27:07.480
is a case that will decide whether same
sex marriage is legal in California.

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:08.600
So whether it's legal or

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:11.070
not there are going to be Californians
who want to marry each other.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:15.630
The question is will we allow them to,
to pursue that transaction.

00:27:15.630 --> 00:27:18.730
Such a repugnant transaction and
the fact that it's changing and

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:23.180
it's likely to keep changing means that
these repugnities keep changing over time.

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:26.270
So think for
instance about interest on loans.

00:27:26.270 --> 00:27:29.460
There were hundreds of years in Europe
where charging interest on loans was

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:30.740
a repugnant transaction.

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:32.580
There were people who wanted loans and

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:35.030
people who would like to
charge interest on them.

00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:37.750
But it wasn't allowed.

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:41.780
And that's changed, and it's had, of
course, profound effects on the economy.

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:45.460
We could hardly have the global
capitalist economy that we have,

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:47.420
if we didn't have a market for capital.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:50.870
So the idea that you
couldn't have a market for

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:56.830
capital had first-order important effects
on how we organize our economic lives.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:28:01.130
But it's not the case that as we get more
modern things that used to be repugnant,

00:28:01.130 --> 00:28:02.020
become less repugnant.

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:03.370
It also goes the other way.

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:05.950
We live in a country where
there used to be markets for

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:08.370
slaves, in the United States.

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:10.045
And that's not a great example because,

00:28:10.045 --> 00:28:13.560
because it's not a voluntary
transaction on the part of the slave.

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:16.070
But the most common form of

00:28:16.070 --> 00:28:19.390
the most common way to buy
passage across the Atlantic Ocean

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:23.420
during the years after the American
revolution was indentured servitude.

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:27.070
There you would be in Ireland or someplace
and you would want to come to Boston.

00:28:27.070 --> 00:28:31.330
And you didn't have the fair to pay
the captain to take you across.

00:28:31.330 --> 00:28:33.770
And so
you would sign articles of indenture.

00:28:33.770 --> 00:28:38.300
And when he got to Boston he would sell
your labor services Unconditional,

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:41.760
you couldn't quit, for five years,
to the highest bidder.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:46.980
And that's a form of involuntary servitude
that is entered into, voluntarily.

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:50.630
But the 13th Amendment to the Constitution
says we don't allow that anymore.

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:54.770
So there are things that used to
be repugnant, and are not anymore.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:59.800
And there are things that used
to not be repugnant and now are.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:01.270
So these are things worth understanding.

00:29:01.270 --> 00:29:04.310
I don't pretend to understand them, but
let me, let's think about them a little,

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.510
because I think they are worth
thinking about and fun to think about.

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:11.110
Why can't you eat horse or
dog meat in a restaurant in California?

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:15.740
You may not want to eat horse or
dog meat in a restaurant in California, so

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:18.270
you might not know that you can't,
but you can't.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:21.200
In fact, well I'll tell you
about the rest of the country,

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:23.710
but the short answer is
it's against the law.

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:25.500
Right?
Horse meat may not be offered for sale for

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:27.270
human consumption it's a felony.

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:28.575
Dog meat is just a misdemeanor.

00:29:28.575 --> 00:29:33.850
[LAUGH]
There's

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:38.470
no law in California against eating
grasshoppers or cockroaches.

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:41.220
Right, by and large you can't eat
them in restaurants either and

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:42.790
that's because no one wants to.

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:45.640
The reason there's a law against eating
horse meat is not because no one wants to,

00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:48.140
it's because some people want to and
other people don't want them to.

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:52.160
So this was actually passed by
the Class of 1998 when they

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:54.300
were here in a referendum.

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:57.530
So you guys got to vote on this
if you were California residents.

00:29:57.530 --> 00:30:00.580
As many people voted for
it It was a referendum.

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:02.510
As many people voted for it as voted for

00:30:02.510 --> 00:30:05.590
Grey Davis who was elected
governor that year.

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:09.310
So again, this is something.

00:30:09.310 --> 00:30:12.850
Because we have a referendum system in
California, there were people who thought

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:15.760
that it was a really bad idea that other
people were eating horse meat and that we

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:20.180
ought to make a law against it, and
through the magic of referendum they did.

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:24.770
Now needless to say, this is something
that varies by time and place.

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.420
There are lots of places in Europe
where you can eat horse meat.

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:29.250
You can eat horse meat
anywhere in the United States,

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:31.390
it's not illegal except in California.

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:34.280
But in order to eat meat in
a restaurant in the United States,

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:38.560
it has to be inspected and
congress has refused to allow the to

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:42.380
provide funds to the US Department of
Agriculture to inspect horse meat.

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:43.320
So although it's not illegal.

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:44.250
It's a crime in California.

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:47.280
It's not a crime in New York, but
no horse meat is available in New York.

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:49.600
You'll have to go to France or Japan.

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:54.010
There are lots of places where
they like horse meat, okay.

00:30:54.010 --> 00:30:55.410
As I have been saying,

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:58.690
transactions vary from place to place
whether they are repugnant or not.

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:00.260
Let's think about dwarf tossing.

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:03.850
You guys are going to go
out drinking tonight,

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:05.735
maybe you've thought about
dwarf tossing already.

00:31:05.735 --> 00:31:10.071
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So his you know, in England,

00:31:10.071 --> 00:31:14.230
it`s legal, and Lenny the giant,
he`s this small man wearing a helmet and

00:31:14.230 --> 00:31:19.270
he earns his living engaging in
an athletic event in which larger and

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:22.070
probably drunker men
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:22.620
&gt;&gt; Throw him for

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:26.080
distance and he lands athletically and
rolls and is wearing safety equipment.

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:31.800
Here's the Ontario Dwarf Tossing Ban Act
of 2003.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It says in Ontario this is

00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:36.640
a repugnant transaction, you can't do it.

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:39.030
And it's not an occupational health and
safety,

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:41.130
it doesn't say Lenny the Giant
should wear a helmet.

00:31:41.130 --> 00:31:43.040
Lenny the Giant is
already wearing a helmet.

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:47.090
It says, no person shall organize a dwarf
tossing event or engage in dwarf tossing.

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:48.850
It basically says,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.490
&gt;&gt; Yuck, you know, you shouldn't do that.

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:58.570
Now A French dwarf,
when France banned dwarf tossing.

00:31:58.570 --> 00:32:03.060
A French dwarf took, who wanted to
continue earning his living this way, so

00:32:03.060 --> 00:32:04.550
it's a repugnant transaction.

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:09.150
There are people who want to engage in it,
and other people who don't want them to,

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:10.810
and that happened in France too.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:14.990
And the French dwarf took his case before
the UN High Commissioner on Human Rights.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:23.020
And he said that his right to
employment was being denied.

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:29.500
And the French case said this is
just sort of yuck, but in French.

00:32:29.500 --> 00:32:32.440
It said this is an affront
to human dignity.

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:40.010
We are all less human when Manuel
Bockenheim earns his living this way.

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:41.280
And he had a very moving statement.

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:42.490
When I read the case materials,

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:46.954
I thought the little guy is going to
win because what he said, is he said,

00:32:46.954 --> 00:32:51.400
his statement said something like, the
essence of human dignity is having a job.

00:32:51.400 --> 00:32:55.269
And there aren't a lot of jobs for
dwarves in France, and this is my job.

00:32:56.640 --> 00:33:00.050
And I remember thinking but
he didn't win, France won.

00:33:00.050 --> 00:33:02.220
The UN committee found for

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:05.680
France, basically saying that every
time he earned his living that way

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:11.020
each of us became a little less human,
and that we shouldn't allow it.

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:13.949
Even though it was
a voluntary transaction.

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:19.740
So repugnant transactions of all sorts,
but it's hard to tell what they are.

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:24.475
It's hard to tell why horse meat is
repugnant in California and not in France.

00:33:24.475 --> 00:33:25.800
because again, it's not the Wild West.

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:28.120
That was a 1998 referendum.

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:30.650
And it's not the small size of the dwarfs.

00:33:30.650 --> 00:33:32.320
Jockeys are small,

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:36.860
we don't mind small guys riding horses and
seeing how fast they can make them go.

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:42.104
And there are things that are even closer
to dwarf tossing like wife carrying.

00:33:42.104 --> 00:33:44.476
So these guys are also athletes.

00:33:44.476 --> 00:33:47.740
They are not married to each other.

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:50.250
Wife carrying is for speed not for

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:51.770
distance but-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:53.269
&gt;&gt; But it's not dignified,

00:33:53.269 --> 00:33:58.520
here at the American championship, they
still use the old-fashioned technique.

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:03.080
But nowadays most people use the Estonian
position and you know particularly for

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:05.480
getting through the water hazards.

00:34:05.480 --> 00:34:10.620
So there's a North American Championship
at Sunday River Ski Resort

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:15.680
each year near Boston and there's a world
championship in Finland each year.

00:34:15.680 --> 00:34:20.500
So I'm not aware of any place in
which wife carrying is repugnant but

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:25.540
I have trouble thinking of why,
I'm not sure it shouldn't be,

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:28.795
but I have trouble thinking of a rule
that would say dwarf tossing is and

00:34:28.795 --> 00:34:32.357
wife carrying isn't or vice versa.

00:34:32.357 --> 00:34:35.850
So although I can't think of general
rules, one thing you notice is that

00:34:35.850 --> 00:34:38.276
adding money to the mix sometimes
makes things repugnant.

00:34:38.276 --> 00:34:43.410
Okay, so
something isn't repugnant by itself,

00:34:43.410 --> 00:34:45.140
but when you add money to it,
it become repugnant.

00:34:45.140 --> 00:34:46.410
So interest on loans.

00:34:46.410 --> 00:34:47.460
Loans weren't repugnant.

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:51.740
People didn't in medieval Europe think it

00:34:51.740 --> 00:34:54.590
was a bad thing to lend someone
else money to start a business.

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:59.270
To buy stock for
a ship to go on a trading mission.

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:03.640
It was charging interest, having your
money earn money that was repugnant.

00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:06.320
Adoption, adoption is expensive.

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:10.140
If you want to adopt a baby it'll
probably cost a lot of money.

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:13.349
But you may not buy the baby
from the birth mother.

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:17.130
Okay, there are some parts of
the transaction that you can't do.

00:35:17.130 --> 00:35:20.820
Prostitution is illegal in many places,
not in all places.

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:23.210
If you look around the world,
then different parts are illegal.

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:27.590
Sometimes brokerage is illegal, pimping
is illegal but prostitution is not.

00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:32.210
But by and large, we're not,
in most of the world we're not,

00:35:32.210 --> 00:35:34.920
against sex,
we're against payment for sex.

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.640
Right?
So it's adding money that

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:42.350
makes those transactions repugnant and
sometimes there are transactions that have

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:45.770
money that aren't repugnant but if there's
too much money it becomes repugnant.

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:48.760
And that's where we think about
laws against price gouging.

00:35:48.760 --> 00:35:50.360
If you're, if there's a blizzard and

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:54.110
you want to raise the price on your
snow shovels at your hardware store,

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:58.480
you'll often find yourself in
violation of local law, of state law.

00:35:58.480 --> 00:36:01.680
And similarly, in Europe right now,
there's talk about limiting the pay of

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:04.930
bankers and, I mean, we have some of
that in the United States as well.

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:07.740
With the idea being that,
money is fine, but

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:11.370
too much money might make a transaction
that we didn't approve of.

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:14.116
Some of this may impact you guys.

00:36:14.116 --> 00:36:20.570
These are people, us, especially those
of us who haven't gone to business and

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:24.990
aren't economists, ordinary people, have
intuitions about economic transactions

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:28.060
that often shape what markets we see and
what we don't.

00:36:28.060 --> 00:36:31.340
And markets require
lots of social support.

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:35.430
So we would be well advised to understand
this intuitions better than we do.

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:42.790
Most people can pick the economist out
from his New Yorker cartoon, right?

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:45.880
So this poor guy, he's come to dinner and

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:47.950
he didn't have time to
get a bottle of wine.

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:52.835
He's trying to offer his hosts
some money and that's, you laugh.

00:36:52.835 --> 00:36:57.690
You know,
if you invited me to dinner at your house,

00:36:57.690 --> 00:37:00.210
the surest thing I could
do that would get me

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:03.410
never invited again is after dinner say,
you know that was a wonderful dinner.

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:05.870
You know that would cost,
you know, $75 in a nice restaurant.

00:37:05.870 --> 00:37:08.310
Can I just pay you for that?

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:09.950
You'd say, this guy doesn't understand.

00:37:09.950 --> 00:37:11.250
We're not a restaurant.

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:13.560
We're not selling food.

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:16.750
Inviting him to dinner at our
house is an offer of friendship.

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:18.910
And he could invite us
to dinner at his house.

00:37:18.910 --> 00:37:19.930
He can invite us back.

00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:22.340
He could bring a nice bottle of wine.

00:37:22.340 --> 00:37:25.700
He could do a lot of things that
would look like a transaction, but

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:27.810
he can't pay for it.

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:32.660
Right, so even those of us
who are very into the economy

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:36.960
understand that there are some things that
would be really offensive to offer to pay.

00:37:38.370 --> 00:37:42.060
Some transactions that can't be monetized.

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:46.710
When you think about why we have laws
against buying and selling kidneys,

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:48.860
there are a couple of arguments.

00:37:48.860 --> 00:37:50.750
One of them,
I've sort of classified them here,

00:37:50.750 --> 00:37:52.540
one of them is just that it is a bad idea.

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:53.500
It's a bad thing.

00:37:53.500 --> 00:37:58.680
People should never be means and
always should be ends.

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:02.190
Many of the Christian churches
adopt that kind of language.

00:38:02.190 --> 00:38:03.520
Another hardest for

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:06.840
economists to understand is they say if
you're offered too much money, it's so

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:10.310
tempting that you do it against your
better judgment, it's a form of coercion.

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:12.410
I have trouble understanding that,
because,

00:38:12.410 --> 00:38:16.080
of course If you offered me $10 million
for my house, you could have it.

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:18.480
I would just feel like I
had to sell it to you.

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:21.315
I would be coerced, but happy.

00:38:21.315 --> 00:38:26.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The arguments that

00:38:26.170 --> 00:38:27.470
I could make the most sense out of, and

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:30.350
have the most sympathy with
are the slippery slope arguments.

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:32.220
We don't have much time today so
I'm not going to go into it, but

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:33.290
those are arguments that say,

00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:36.890
if you start with allowing people to
sell their kidneys, you might end up

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:40.500
living in a less sympathetic society
than you would like to live in.

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:42.300
And I think those have to
be taken very seriously,

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:44.100
and we have to think about them.

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:49.680
So let me get a sense of the meeting,
test yourself.

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:51.920
For repugnance I'm going to ask for
a show of hands.

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:56.930
Are you willing to contemplate carefully
regulated sales of live kidneys?

00:38:56.930 --> 00:38:57.850
Put up your hand if you are.

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:01.000
Okay, keep it up because look around.

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:01.830
Lots of you, right?

00:39:01.830 --> 00:39:02.543
MBA reunion.

00:39:02.543 --> 00:39:04.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Keep it up, keep it up.

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:08.360
How many of you who are willing to
contemplate the sales of live kidneys

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:11.070
would be willing to contemplate
the sales of live hearts?

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:12.285
Kills the seller.

00:39:12.285 --> 00:39:17.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Voluntary, okay?

00:39:17.500 --> 00:39:23.070
So my point is Lots of us have
our hands up for kidneys,

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:27.570
which means maybe that we feel less of
this repugnance than the general public.

00:39:27.570 --> 00:39:28.690
But many of you,

00:39:28.690 --> 00:39:34.110
not all of you until I bullied you,
start to pull your hands down for hearts.

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:39.560
Which means we all have places that we're
at least willing to understand The idea

00:39:39.560 --> 00:39:43.660
that some transactions might be repugnant
even if they were willingly entered into.

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:48.480
And therefore, it behooves us to try
to understand where other people draw

00:39:48.480 --> 00:39:51.550
those lines, even when they draw
them in different places than we do.

00:39:51.550 --> 00:39:55.400
Right, so you may think kidney
sales would be a good idea, but

00:39:55.400 --> 00:39:58.350
in order to have this kind of discussion
we're going to have to seriously engage

00:39:58.350 --> 00:40:00.700
with people who think that kidney
sales would be a terrible idea.

00:40:00.700 --> 00:40:04.230
A bad idea, the kind of bad
idea that only bad people have.

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:07.435
Okay and I meet lots of those people.

00:40:07.435 --> 00:40:11.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So kidney exchange achieves

00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:15.370
some of the benefits of a market without
running into this barrier of repugnance.

00:40:15.370 --> 00:40:18.320
So the thing we've achieved with
kidneys as far as market design is

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:19.400
we're very pragmatic.

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:23.980
We say you can't sell kidneys, we can
debate that till the cows come home but

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:26.880
in the meantime what are we going to do
for the patients, and what we're able to

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:31.750
do is get the benefits of exchange without
running into the barriers of repugnance.

00:40:32.790 --> 00:40:37.510
So let me just close with a thought
on what is a free market?

00:40:37.510 --> 00:40:41.810
Because here I am designing markets and
that can seem like a funny thing.

00:40:41.810 --> 00:40:45.880
And I think the right metaphor to look at
is a free market isn't a market without

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:49.520
rules it's a market with rules
that allow it to operate freely.

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:53.090
The New York stock exchange has lots of
rules, and some of them are laws that if

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:56.540
you violate you can go to jail,
rules against insider trading.

00:40:56.540 --> 00:41:00.700
And they're designed to help make it
a very efficient collaborated market.

00:41:00.700 --> 00:41:04.190
So I think a good metaphor is you think
of a wheel that could rotate freely.

00:41:04.190 --> 00:41:08.230
It rotates freely because it has
an axle and well-oiled bearings,

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:10.960
and that's what lets it go.

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:14.720
So I think the political
discussion on free market is often

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:17.430
that free markets are laissez-faire,
they don't have rules at all.

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:19.170
But very few markets are like that.

00:41:19.170 --> 00:41:22.670
When you get jobs, when you apply for
colleges, when you buy and

00:41:22.670 --> 00:41:24.700
sell stocks on
the New York Stock Exchange,

00:41:24.700 --> 00:41:26.750
you're engaging in markets
that have a lot of rules.

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:29.390
And if if the markets are designed well,

00:41:29.390 --> 00:41:32.600
then the rules allow
the market to work well.

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:37.890
So a big part of that of course
is is handing out information.

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:40.350
Moving it around and helping aggregate it.

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:44.010
So I was advised that I should end
by telling you some stories and

00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:47.750
giving you some advice having
to do with the Nobel prize so

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:50.410
here is a picture of me and
the king of Sweden.

00:41:50.410 --> 00:41:53.010
I'm the guy on the left.

00:41:53.010 --> 00:41:58.330
[LAUGH] Let me tell you a story,
you hear about the Nobel

00:41:58.330 --> 00:42:02.130
prize in a phone call in the middle of
the night if you live in California.

00:42:02.130 --> 00:42:05.140
But the guys giving the phone call
have some experience at this.

00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:09.505
And it turns out, the thing that they're
really concerned with, is that you

00:42:09.505 --> 00:42:13.530
shouldn't think its a hoax, so they
call you up in the middle of the night.

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:16.280
We actually missed the first phone call,
my wife said I think the phone's ringing

00:42:16.280 --> 00:42:18.856
and I woke up and it wasn't ringing but
they called back.

00:42:18.856 --> 00:42:24.480
And the guy on the phone says,

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:28.930
and I still don't know who it was who,
waive the initial cost, Swedish accent.

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:30.690
And he said you've won the Nobel Prize.

00:42:30.690 --> 00:42:33.110
He said but
I'm here with six of my colleagues and

00:42:33.110 --> 00:42:34.640
two of them are people you know.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:37.440
And they're now going to tell you.

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:40.630
So that was their way of I guess

00:42:40.630 --> 00:42:45.120
forestalling that you should say come on,
who is this?

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:45.876
Is that Ted?

00:42:45.876 --> 00:42:46.403
[LAUGH] You know?

00:42:46.403 --> 00:42:50.070
[LAUGH] And they did that
actually at 3:30 and they said so

00:42:50.070 --> 00:42:56.065
it's not going to be announced for
half an hour yet, keep it a secret.

00:42:56.065 --> 00:42:58.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And what I did in that half hour and

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:00.830
this is my advice,
well I didn't have half hour.

00:43:00.830 --> 00:43:06.050
I took a shower and I got dressed and if
you're ever in that situation, you should

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:10.470
definitely do that because there isn't any
time afterward for the next many hours.

00:43:10.470 --> 00:43:11.810
It was a busy day.

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:15.040
Fun, but not relaxing.

00:43:15.040 --> 00:43:18.450
So, it's great for
me to be back at Stanford.

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:20.420
I welcome you back to Stanford.

00:43:20.420 --> 00:43:23.990
And there was one slide I skipped,
let me just tell you about that.

00:43:23.990 --> 00:43:24.810
What do markets do?

00:43:24.810 --> 00:43:27.080
What do market places do for markets?

00:43:27.080 --> 00:43:29.640
They have to make the market thick.

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:32.200
They have to bring enough
people together to make

00:43:33.430 --> 00:43:35.920
the transactions that will
make the market work.

00:43:35.920 --> 00:43:38.090
And once you have those people
together sometimes it's hard to do

00:43:38.090 --> 00:43:38.830
the transactions.

00:43:38.830 --> 00:43:40.690
With kidneys it's because
they operate in rooms but

00:43:40.690 --> 00:43:43.240
you have a lot of
transactions to consider.

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:46.200
So markets have to become
uncongested to work well and

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:51.480
they have to be safe to participate in and
some kinds of transactions are repugnant.

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:54.490
So I think when you go back
out into the market place,

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:58.350
if you about these things you'll
get a chance to see markets and

00:43:58.350 --> 00:44:02.680
market institutions in a little bit of
a different way and that's a good thing.

00:44:04.530 --> 00:44:05.165
So thank you very much.

00:44:05.165 --> 00:44:14.961
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:44:14.961 --> 00:44:16.745
&gt;&gt; I've used up our time, but

00:44:16.745 --> 00:44:20.478
I am willing to take questions,
if there are questions.

00:44:20.478 --> 00:44:20.978
Are there microphones around?

00:44:25.747 --> 00:44:26.390
Here you go.

00:44:26.390 --> 00:44:32.446
There's people with microphones to.

00:44:32.446 --> 00:44:33.500
&gt;&gt; It's live, yeah.

00:44:33.500 --> 00:44:34.850
Thank you, that was fascinating.

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:35.580
I'm just curious,

00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:39.910
you said there's no contracts because then
it would be value consideration, etc.

00:44:39.910 --> 00:44:43.330
But you have a chain of 60 people, so

00:44:43.330 --> 00:44:46.390
somewhere along the line you
get a guy who goes, we're good.

00:44:46.390 --> 00:44:47.640
you know.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:49.190
&gt;&gt; Yes, absolutely.

00:44:49.190 --> 00:44:53.110
So the non simultaneous donors, so

00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:56.310
we do them in batches and
then there's bridge donors.

00:44:56.310 --> 00:45:00.270
And the nurse social workers
transplant coordinators they talk

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:03.580
to these people beforehand and they don't
nominate everyone to be a bridge donor.

00:45:03.580 --> 00:45:06.394
Our reneg rate,
our broken link rate is 2%.

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:11.580
So one of the things economists
are learning is this thing called

00:45:11.580 --> 00:45:12.240
behavioral economics.

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:15.130
One of the things economists are learning
is people are a lot nicer than we

00:45:15.130 --> 00:45:16.630
give them credit for.

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:19.240
But, this is a more complicated
transaction than that.

00:45:19.240 --> 00:45:24.390
Supposing it's costly, it's a big deal,
but let's think about it.

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:26.540
Supposing you are going to
be the bridge donor.

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:28.460
Why are you the bridge donor?

00:45:28.460 --> 00:45:31.870
Well, you had signed up to give
your sister, or your wife, or

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:36.780
your mother a kidney and you weren't
able to but because of your willingness,

00:45:36.780 --> 00:45:39.120
your sister gets a kidney.

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:42.270
And now, you've agreed that next Tuesday
at 5:30 in the morning you'll show up at

00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:43.399
the OR and give a kidney.

00:45:45.470 --> 00:45:48.290
Future Thanksgivings at your
family are going to remember

00:45:48.290 --> 00:45:49.600
how you saved your sister's life.

00:45:51.320 --> 00:45:55.990
The discussion will be a lot funnier if
you didn't give the kidney in the end so,

00:45:55.990 --> 00:45:58.890
you know, those Thanksgivings would.

00:45:58.890 --> 00:45:59.970
That would be a different story.

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:02.960
So I think,
I'm not quite sure what goes into it,

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:07.380
but once the transplant coordinators
say we've talked to this person and

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:09.960
he's good to go, they almost always are.

00:46:12.530 --> 00:46:16.190
&gt;&gt; Could you talk a little bit about
the key elements as you see them for

00:46:16.190 --> 00:46:18.850
market design for school choice.

00:46:18.850 --> 00:46:25.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well so thickness has to do
with getting all the schools involved.

00:46:26.170 --> 00:46:28.940
Some schools sometimes have some
volition and if it's a simple district,

00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:34.450
school district, they can command all
the schools and then this isn't a problem.

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:36.990
But if there are a lot of charter
schools and things like that

00:46:36.990 --> 00:46:39.670
they sometimes have to be coordinated so
that you can get all the schools together.

00:46:39.670 --> 00:46:42.340
So that you can deal with congestion.

00:46:42.340 --> 00:46:46.970
Because what's congestion
in a school choice system?

00:46:46.970 --> 00:46:51.420
It's many children having multiple offers
while many more don't have any offers

00:46:51.420 --> 00:46:55.050
because multiple offers are being held and
wait lists are being formed.

00:46:55.050 --> 00:46:59.030
So when we looked at this in New York City
initially for New York City high schools,

00:46:59.030 --> 00:47:02.890
we found that 17,000 children a year
were getting multiple offers.

00:47:04.950 --> 00:47:08.540
And about thirty thousand at the end of
the process had to be put into schools to

00:47:08.540 --> 00:47:12.010
which they had expressed
no preferences just because

00:47:12.010 --> 00:47:13.860
time ran out on the waiting list.

00:47:13.860 --> 00:47:16.430
So we looked at the seventeen thousand
who were getting multiple offers and

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:19.010
we found they have been asked for
preferences before hand.

00:47:19.010 --> 00:47:22.790
We found that they almost invariably took
the school that they said they liked best.

00:47:22.790 --> 00:47:25.500
So one of the things we did is
we organized the systems so

00:47:25.500 --> 00:47:26.690
that they now only get one offer.

00:47:26.690 --> 00:47:29.590
They get an offer from the school
that they ranked highest

00:47:29.590 --> 00:47:30.930
that's willing to accept them.

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:33.570
And that allows a lot more
students to get offers.

00:47:33.570 --> 00:47:36.660
Another thing that's going on
is the market has to be safe.

00:47:36.660 --> 00:47:39.450
So in the city of Boston

00:47:39.450 --> 00:47:42.761
they had an existing school choice
system that sounded very nice.

00:47:42.761 --> 00:47:46.690
They tried to give as many people
as possible their first choice.

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:50.070
The problem with that is if you
didn't get your first choice

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:53.888
It was very likely that your second choice
was going to be full of people who had

00:47:53.888 --> 00:47:55.554
listed it as their first choice.

00:47:55.554 --> 00:47:58.824
So it became very important to think
carefully what you should list as your

00:47:58.824 --> 00:47:59.700
first choice.

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:02.204
You couldn't just list your first
choice as your first choice.

00:48:02.204 --> 00:48:03.985
So we now made it safe for
them to do that.

00:48:03.985 --> 00:48:05.815
We now made it safe in Boston so

00:48:05.815 --> 00:48:10.530
that families can list the schools in
the order in which they prefer them.

00:48:10.530 --> 00:48:13.590
And if they don't get their first choice,
they'll have just as much chance of

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:16.360
getting their second choice as
if they had listed it first.

00:48:16.360 --> 00:48:19.500
And that lifts a big burden
of strategizing for families.

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:23.323
And it also gives the school system
good data on what schools people like.

00:48:23.323 --> 00:48:26.040
So those are the kinds of market
design things that we think about.

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:31.346
And we've been able to do them in
a couple of American cities now.

00:48:31.346 --> 00:48:34.164
Yes you have a microphone for
this gentleman?

00:48:34.164 --> 00:48:34.767
Here.

00:48:34.767 --> 00:48:36.245
&gt;&gt; You can go next.

00:48:36.245 --> 00:48:37.540
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:39.490
&gt;&gt; Go, go ahead.

00:48:39.490 --> 00:48:40.610
&gt;&gt; Someone has a microphone already?

00:48:40.610 --> 00:48:41.514
&gt;&gt; Yes I have a microphone.

00:48:41.514 --> 00:48:42.833
&gt;&gt; Wave your hand I can't see you.

00:48:42.833 --> 00:48:44.082
Thank you.

00:48:44.082 --> 00:48:44.901
&gt;&gt; Thank you for coming.

00:48:44.901 --> 00:48:49.593
I find this so fascinating as economist
by training, I love sort of optimization

00:48:49.593 --> 00:48:54.009
problem in it all but I think one thing,
the next step I have a question in it in

00:48:54.009 --> 00:48:58.226
all is understandability of the system for
everyone who participates.

00:48:58.226 --> 00:49:01.554
Because I think that while ideally any
optimal solution would be very elegant,

00:49:01.554 --> 00:49:03.540
I imagine sometimes they're quite complex.

00:49:03.540 --> 00:49:07.236
And in some markets that may not matter,
but somethings like the public school

00:49:07.236 --> 00:49:09.935
assignment people are sort
of unwilling participants.

00:49:09.935 --> 00:49:10.932
They don't have-
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:49:10.932 --> 00:49:11.760
&gt;&gt; Choose to be part of it.

00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:12.740
They have to be part of it, and

00:49:12.740 --> 00:49:13.983
if they don't understand it,
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:49:13.983 --> 00:49:15.550
&gt;&gt; There may be broken trust.

00:49:15.550 --> 00:49:17.093
There may be confusion.

00:49:17.093 --> 00:49:20.300
So how does that both go into how you
design the market, and how does what

00:49:20.300 --> 00:49:24.013
are the implications when you are rolling
it out and trying to make it successful?

00:49:24.013 --> 00:49:28.360
&gt;&gt; That's a giant consideration, and
what we've come to understand is that

00:49:28.360 --> 00:49:32.849
communicating about how the system works
is part of the design of the system.

00:49:32.849 --> 00:49:36.662
So we've started to tell school districts
it's not just that we'll help you design

00:49:36.662 --> 00:49:40.315
an algorithm that has good properties but
we'll help you design an algorithm and

00:49:40.315 --> 00:49:43.698
a communication package that will help
convey it because indeed we had some

00:49:43.698 --> 00:49:45.170
startup problems of this sort.

00:49:45.170 --> 00:49:46.820
So there are two ways,

00:49:46.820 --> 00:49:50.310
there a couple of ways that a school
choice system can be complex.

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:54.350
I just described to you the old system
used in Boston and it was simple.

00:49:54.350 --> 00:49:55.380
I was able to describe it to you.

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:58.450
It was simple to describe, but
it was complex to participate in

00:49:58.450 --> 00:50:01.860
because you couldn't just put down your
first choice as your first choice.

00:50:01.860 --> 00:50:04.550
You had to know all sorts of
things about the schools.

00:50:04.550 --> 00:50:07.740
For instance, Boston schools
would give sibling priority to

00:50:08.760 --> 00:50:11.320
children who had an older
sib in the school.

00:50:11.320 --> 00:50:16.021
And so parents who had children about
to enter kindergarten, if they were in

00:50:16.021 --> 00:50:21.459
prosperous, well informed, highly educated
neighborhoods, they would develop and

00:50:21.459 --> 00:50:25.587
intelligence network that knew how
many sibs there were going in.

00:50:25.587 --> 00:50:27.840
So they'd know how many
places were in each school.

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:30.927
It was hard work.

00:50:30.927 --> 00:50:34.231
So we got a system that's a little
more complicated to describe but

00:50:34.231 --> 00:50:35.591
it's very simple to play.

00:50:35.591 --> 00:50:38.768
You just decide which schools you like and

00:50:38.768 --> 00:50:43.874
it's safe to tell about in public
schools which schools you like.

00:50:43.874 --> 00:50:47.478
So, the system that we use Is
not that hard to describe,

00:50:47.478 --> 00:50:50.702
it's based on the deferred
acceptance algorithm

00:50:50.702 --> 00:50:55.093
that Lloyd Chapley just got
the Nobel Prize for, he and I shared it.

00:50:55.093 --> 00:50:57.422
It's pretty simple to describe,
I won't do it right now.

00:50:57.422 --> 00:51:02.145
It's more complicated to describe than
trying to give everyone their first choice

00:51:02.145 --> 00:51:06.880
and then give as many people as possible
their second choice in that system.

00:51:06.880 --> 00:51:08.520
But that system is hard to play.

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:12.250
So I think what happens is we
communicate what's going on,

00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:14.190
some people understand it.

00:51:14.190 --> 00:51:18.205
It's enough, though, if other people just
get the message that it's safe to put down

00:51:18.205 --> 00:51:19.367
your true preferences.

00:51:22.812 --> 00:51:25.924
&gt;&gt; Well thank you, Professor,
this has been fascinating.

00:51:25.924 --> 00:51:30.509
You must find it very rewarding to have
created work that saved thousands of

00:51:30.509 --> 00:51:31.031
lives.

00:51:31.031 --> 00:51:34.069
Can I ask where else you'd
like to see this work applied,

00:51:34.069 --> 00:51:36.043
what else you'd like to transform?

00:51:36.043 --> 00:51:39.016
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:51:39.016 --> 00:51:42.481
So we started with these sort of
centralized marketplaces, not because

00:51:42.481 --> 00:51:46.340
those are the most common, or whatever,
but because those are the easiest to get

00:51:46.340 --> 00:51:49.840
your arms around and see where
the design is, where the rules are.

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:52.840
But most marketplaces operate
on decentralized rules.

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:56.800
And in some of the marketplaces we've
worked on, later career doctors in

00:51:56.800 --> 00:52:00.840
various specialties for instance, what
we've done is we've change the rules of

00:52:00.840 --> 00:52:05.070
engagement in decentralized markets,
rules about the market culture.

00:52:05.070 --> 00:52:10.228
How an offer of employment should be made.

00:52:10.228 --> 00:52:12.530
How long it should be left open.

00:52:12.530 --> 00:52:15.320
Whether people can change their
minds under what circumstances.

00:52:15.320 --> 00:52:19.270
So having rules like that in decentralized
markets is part of their design.

00:52:19.270 --> 00:52:22.330
And it's part of their culture,
it's a soft part of the world.

00:52:22.330 --> 00:52:26.598
Often when you actually see markets
operating there are conventions about that

00:52:26.598 --> 00:52:30.928
that make the market work well or badly,
but there may not be trade organizations

00:52:30.928 --> 00:52:34.322
that can provide guidelines,
although sometimes there are.

00:52:34.322 --> 00:52:39.040
So I think that we need to do more about
thinking about decentralized markets.

00:52:39.040 --> 00:52:41.320
We also, as a profession,

00:52:41.320 --> 00:52:44.520
economists have spent a lot of time
thinking about the price of transactions.

00:52:44.520 --> 00:52:47.100
But not very much time
thinking about their timing.

00:52:47.100 --> 00:52:49.680
So for instance now when we
look at securities markets and

00:52:49.680 --> 00:52:53.000
high speed trading there
are questions about whether

00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:56.860
would we really loss efficiency if instead
of being able to trade at any time,

00:52:56.860 --> 00:53:02.730
you could only trade very second on the
second, but that's a tinny change, right?

00:53:02.730 --> 00:53:07.240
Because there is very little real economic
information that happens in a second, but

00:53:07.240 --> 00:53:12.200
of course high speed traders who
can trade in milliseconds, they can

00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:16.980
force liquidity providers to have bigger
spreads because they can get information,

00:53:16.980 --> 00:53:20.030
earlier and trade against stale
spreads and things like that.

00:53:20.030 --> 00:53:21.930
So I think questions like that, you know,

00:53:21.930 --> 00:53:25.990
how should we have to be thinking about
the microstructure of financial markets

00:53:25.990 --> 00:53:27.740
are things we'll surely
have to think about.

00:53:29.630 --> 00:53:33.650
I see market design everywhere so
if you type market sign in Google,

00:53:33.650 --> 00:53:35.740
you'll find my blog by
the name of market design.

00:53:35.740 --> 00:53:37.480
And I make a post almost everyday.

00:53:37.480 --> 00:53:40.170
And some of them are not very germane,
but basically,

00:53:40.170 --> 00:53:41.950
just because I see market
design everywhere.

00:53:41.950 --> 00:53:44.080
I started it for a class, and
I just wanted them to know,

00:53:44.080 --> 00:53:49.780
that I could stand up here and tell you
things that could use market design a lot.

00:53:49.780 --> 00:53:51.650
But you type market design into Google,

00:53:51.650 --> 00:53:54.412
you'll see the kinds of things
I've been concerned with.

00:53:54.412 --> 00:53:56.446
One in the back.

00:53:56.446 --> 00:53:59.162
&gt;&gt; Thank you for that insightful talk.

00:53:59.162 --> 00:54:02.174
Do you think some of the theories that-
&gt;&gt; Wave your hand, I can't see you.

00:54:02.174 --> 00:54:03.565
&gt;&gt; I'm here.

00:54:03.565 --> 00:54:05.695
&gt;&gt; I got you, okay.
&gt;&gt; Do you think some of the market

00:54:05.695 --> 00:54:09.617
matching theories are more applicable
in emerging markets where there

00:54:09.617 --> 00:54:11.495
are institutional gaps?

00:54:11.495 --> 00:54:18.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think for development economics
I think market design is a big thing.

00:54:18.040 --> 00:54:20.900
I talked about commodity markets
at the beginning of my talk, and

00:54:20.900 --> 00:54:24.160
I said commodity markets are easy,
price decides who gets what.

00:54:24.160 --> 00:54:28.423
But of course,
commodity markets have to be designed.

00:54:28.423 --> 00:54:32.460
God designed weed, but
wheat isn't a commodity.

00:54:32.460 --> 00:54:36.983
Number two red winter weedt is a
commodity, and number two red winter weed,

00:54:36.983 --> 00:54:40.674
God didn't make that,
the Chicago Board of Trade made that.

00:54:40.674 --> 00:54:44.865
Now why is it good to number two
red winter weed as a commodity?

00:54:44.865 --> 00:54:47.906
Well, wheat is different,
different farmers grow different stuffs.

00:54:47.906 --> 00:54:50.100
And before we had a commodity market for
wheat.

00:54:50.100 --> 00:54:53.720
You had to know your farmer, you had
to have someone go out in the field and

00:54:53.720 --> 00:54:54.240
inspect it.

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:56.090
How much white weed is
mixed with the red weed?

00:54:56.090 --> 00:54:56.820
How much moisture?

00:54:56.820 --> 00:54:57.590
How many bags?

00:54:59.110 --> 00:55:02.820
So once you have a commodity and
an enforceable standards.

00:55:02.820 --> 00:55:06.260
You can all of sudden not care who the
farmer is, the air loads can come by and

00:55:06.260 --> 00:55:08.920
they can take the number
two red winter weed and

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:12.360
mix it together in the rails cars and
not care who it came from but

00:55:12.360 --> 00:55:15.290
before that the mark for
weed was a matching market.

00:55:15.290 --> 00:55:17.900
So Ethiopia has just
made a commodity market,

00:55:17.900 --> 00:55:20.790
a good one, a better one for coffee.

00:55:20.790 --> 00:55:25.410
So, it used to be if you wanted to buy
coffee you had to send your man to Atticus

00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:27.480
and he would taste coffee.

00:55:27.480 --> 00:55:30.970
You know, he would reach the long handle
spoon into big bags of coffee and

00:55:30.970 --> 00:55:35.130
pull out some beans and
grind them and taste them and

00:55:35.130 --> 00:55:38.490
decide whether he wanted to
buy coffee from this farmer.

00:55:38.490 --> 00:55:43.020
Well, now they got this system of market
design where there's anonymous tasting.

00:55:43.020 --> 00:55:46.690
There are guys who Who receive
bags of coffee and sample them and

00:55:46.690 --> 00:55:51.890
don't know who's the farmer and they can
make reliable judgements about quality.

00:55:51.890 --> 00:55:55.670
Turns out, what do I know about this,
because of this I've learned about coffee.

00:55:55.670 --> 00:55:57.860
It turns out what makes
high quality coffee,

00:55:57.860 --> 00:56:01.630
among other things,
is waiting until the cherry is red.

00:56:01.630 --> 00:56:06.250
So a coffee bush has a cherry on it,
and the bean is inside.

00:56:06.250 --> 00:56:09.900
So by the time someone has sold you
the beans you can't see whether they

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:11.140
waited till the cherry was ripe.

00:56:11.140 --> 00:56:13.990
Whether it was red or
whether it was green.

00:56:13.990 --> 00:56:17.630
So the cheap way to pick coffee is you go
to some hillside that's mostly red and

00:56:17.630 --> 00:56:19.910
you say to your pickers,
go pick all the beans.

00:56:19.910 --> 00:56:22.490
We're not coming back here, get them all.

00:56:22.490 --> 00:56:27.118
But once you have tasters who can tell
that you've got unripe beans together with

00:56:27.118 --> 00:56:29.431
your ripe ones, you say to the pickers,

00:56:29.431 --> 00:56:32.785
pick only the red cherries and
we'll come back next week.

00:56:32.785 --> 00:56:34.890
And we'll pick the rest, when they're red.

00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:37.000
So just having a commodity market,

00:56:37.000 --> 00:56:42.720
not only does it let places like
Starbucks buy by the ton grade A coffee.

00:56:42.720 --> 00:56:46.740
But it causes there to be more
grade A coffee because you now get

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:52.550
paid back when you take the trouble
to make your coffee grade A.

00:56:52.550 --> 00:56:55.289
So, I'm not quite sure that's the kind
of thing you were thinking of.

00:56:55.289 --> 00:56:57.554
But when I think about emerging markets,

00:56:57.554 --> 00:57:01.446
you think about how to make the markets
work so that they'll be able to.

00:57:01.446 --> 00:57:05.990
They'll be designed properly to work well
for the conditions that they have there.

00:57:08.190 --> 00:57:09.630
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:57:09.630 --> 00:57:12.858
I'm really interested in
the online currency BitCoin, and

00:57:12.858 --> 00:57:15.209
I was wondering if you'd
had a look at this.

00:57:15.209 --> 00:57:19.220
And whether there are any aspects
of that market that you think

00:57:19.220 --> 00:57:23.863
are fundamentally unworkable, or
if you do think it's sustainable.

00:57:23.863 --> 00:57:26.210
&gt;&gt; So
I don't know much about BitCoin except for

00:57:26.210 --> 00:57:28.230
what I've read in the newspaper.

00:57:28.230 --> 00:57:31.270
It's sort of a cross between
paper money and gold, right?

00:57:31.270 --> 00:57:33.750
It's a little like gold
because it's hard to mine,

00:57:33.750 --> 00:57:36.680
that it's hard to increase the supply.

00:57:36.680 --> 00:57:39.810
That's, I think its advocates
think of that as a good thing, but

00:57:39.810 --> 00:57:43.100
of course, that's not a good thing for
an economy-wide currency,

00:57:43.100 --> 00:57:45.470
because as the economy grows,
you need more currency.

00:57:45.470 --> 00:57:48.500
And if it's hard to get,
there can be shortages.

00:57:48.500 --> 00:57:51.449
But it's also like paper money,
in the sense that

00:57:53.120 --> 00:57:56.820
you can't hold it in your hand,
you can't use it as a commodity later.

00:57:56.820 --> 00:58:00.970
You can't use it to fill teeth or
to make jewelry.

00:58:00.970 --> 00:58:01.990
It's fiat money,

00:58:01.990 --> 00:58:05.060
it's only money as long as people
are willing to accept it as money.

00:58:05.060 --> 00:58:09.340
So I don't see it, and it's pretty
interesting, the fact that you have to

00:58:09.340 --> 00:58:14.461
solve computationally hard problems
in order to create a new BitCoin and

00:58:14.461 --> 00:58:16.600
that this something that can be verified.

00:58:16.600 --> 00:58:20.796
It is a fascinating example of
an attempt to make a currency.

00:58:20.796 --> 00:58:23.883
But I can't see it becoming
a large part of world economy and

00:58:23.883 --> 00:58:25.816
partly it's because of the limits.

00:58:25.816 --> 00:58:30.870
Gold can't be that large a part of
the world economy for the same reason.

00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:36.050
Right, you don't want, here we are talking
about what central banks do and

00:58:36.050 --> 00:58:39.060
how much unemployment should
go with how much inflation.

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:40.797
Gold doesn't let you do
any of those things.

00:58:44.545 --> 00:58:49.120
Commodity money made a lot of sense
when we couldn't do better than that.

00:58:49.120 --> 00:58:51.860
So BitCoin is almost a commodity money is,

00:58:51.860 --> 00:58:55.640
except it's not even a commodity, so
it's fascinating stuff from what I read.

00:58:55.640 --> 00:58:58.745
I wouldn't bet the whole ranch on it.

00:58:58.745 --> 00:59:03.159
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:59:03.159 --> 00:59:07.017
I think that's all the time we have for
questions.

00:59:07.017 --> 00:59:08.632
&gt;&gt; Okay, well, thank you.

00:59:08.632 --> 00:59:10.019
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:59:10.019 --> 00:59:15.150
[MUSIC]

